= Solutions & Biomolecules

Determining the intermolecular forces in a sample:
- Are ions present?
  - Ion-dipole
  - Ion-induced dipole
- Are dipoles present?
  - Dipole-dipole
  - Dipole-induced dipole
- Can molecules form hydrogen bonds?
  - N-H, O-H, F-H plus lone pairs
- Dispersion (London) forces are always present

== Solute Reduces Vapor Pressure of Solution

The vapor pressure of a solution containing a *nonvolatile electrolyte* is always *lower* than the vapor pressure of the pure solvent.

== Dual Polarity and Effects on Solubility

- Alcohols are organic compounds that have *dual polarity*.
- The *-OH* group of an alcohol is polar.
  - It interacts with water through *H bonds*
  - With nonpolar molecules through weak *dipole-induced dipole* forces
- The *hydrocarbon* portion is nonpolar.
  - It interacts through weak *dipole-induced dipole* forces with water and through *dispersion* forces with hexane.


Molecule with larger portion of polar bonds are usually more soluble in water.

== "Like Dissolves Like" Rule

- Substances that exhibit *similar types of intermolecular force* dissolve in each other.
- When a solution forms, *solute-solute* attractions and *solvent-solvent* attractions are replaced by *solute-solvent* attractions.
- This can only occur if the forces within the *solute* and *solvent* are *similar* to the forces that replace them.

== Amino Acids and Proteins

- Natural *proteins* are linear polymers or *polypeptides* composed of any of the 20 types of amino acid monomers linked together by *peptide bonds*.
- *Amino acid* = monomer unit of a protein polymer = organic compound with at least one carboxyl and one amine group on the same molecule.
- Proteins or polypeptides can be either natural or synthetic.

== IMFs in Proteins

Amino acids can be classified by their 20 different side chains. They influences all protein structures.

- *Primary structure*
  - describes the sequence of amino acids in the polymer chain
  - *protein backbone* is structurally same across all natural proteins
  - peptide bonds (in protein backbone) can provide H-bonds
- *Secondary structure*: beta sheets, helixes
  - influenced by the *protein backbone* and *side chains*
  - IMFs = primarily *stabilized* by H-bonds
- *Tertiary structure*
  - IMFs = H-bonds, ionic salt bridges, dispersion, S-S covalent bonds,...
- *Quaternary structure*
  - IMFs = dispersion, H-bonds, ionic bridges, dipole, S-S covalent bonds,..

Water soluble proteins have polar/ionic exteriors and nonpolar interiors.

== Nucleus Acids and DNA

- Nucleic acids (DNA and RNA) are unbranched polynucleotide polymers consisting of four different types of mononucleotides.
- Mononucleotide = unit of nucleic acid consisting of nitrogen base, sugar, and phosphate group.
- H bond is key to DNA replication.
- The base sequence determines the sequence of amino acids.
